    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d148">

                <pb facs="0334" n="306" sameAs="frus:frus1955-57v03/pg306"/>

                <head>148. Telegram From Ambassador U.
                        Alexis Johnson to the Department of
                        State<anchor n="1" xml:id="d148-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Geneva</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1956-02-09T16:00:00+01:00"><hi rendition="#italic">February 9,
                                1956—4 p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>1474. 1. Four hour fifty minute meeting this morning. No progress whatsoever.
                    Only unusual development Wang took twenty
                    minute recess immediately following my opening statement.<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d148-f2"/> Although I received impression this was for purpose
                    conferring on whether my opening statement fitted in with some contingent action
                    they had planned, subsequent developments in meeting gave no indication as to
                    what it might have been.</p>
                <p>2. My general impression is that while no sign whatever any shift their position,
                    threat of break on renunciation force has somewhat receded.</p>
                <p>3. He was prepared omit any discussion implementation today but in response my
                    initiative again took strong line on Liu Yungming, <pb
                        facs="0335" n="307" xml:id="pg307"/> Taiwan entry permits, lists of Chinese
                    US prisons, alleged US attempt force application for permanent residence,
                    etc.</p>
                <p>4. Because of spring festival he asked for next meeting Feb. 20 and when I
                    hesitated, suggested Feb. 18 which I accepted.</p>
                <p>5. Departing for Prague tomorrow morning.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#bold">[Johnson]</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d148-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d148-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Department of State, Central Files, 611.93/2–956. Confidential; Niact.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d148-f2" n="2" xml:id="d148-f2-content">
                <p>Johnson transmitted the text of the
                    statement, which concerned renunciation of force, in his detailed report of the
                    meeting in telegram 1479 from Geneva, February 9. (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Ibid.</hi>)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
